   <dc:rights>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Â© WFP/Gabriela Vivacqua</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:rights>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">WFPâ€™s Cash for Asset participants posing for a portrait in a rice farm in Twic County, Warrap state.&#xA;&#xA;Climate shocks, conflict and rising costs have driven millions of people in need of humanitarian assistance in South Sudan. In the past three years, record floods have washed away entire villages, destroyed livelihoods and displaced hundreds of thousands of people.&#xA;&#xA;South Sudan is facing the hungriest year ever with more than sixty percent of the population or 7.74 million people - including 2 million children and pregnant and nursing mothers struggling to survive the peak of the lean season.&#xA;&#xA;In 2021, WFP reached 5.9 million food insecure people with lifesaving food and nutrition assistance including nearly 800,000 flood-affected people in the country.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
  </rdf:Description>
 </rdf:RDF>
