     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">&lt;p&gt;Florence is a 16 years old grade 8 student whom, before benefiting from the Home Grown School Feeding program would walked 1.5 hours back home during break time to eat. This was not only stressful for her but it also reduced her ability to concentrate on her education as she was constantly tired. Thanks to WFP&amp;rsquo;s school feeding programme, she now benefits from a hot meal per day and nutritious milk produced by local farmers supported by WFP, permiting her to concentrate on her studies. Instead of going home for lunch, she reviews her lessons at school and returns late in the afternoon.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Since 2013, the WFP initiated the "Home Grown School Feeding" in which the food commodities used in this program are purchased locally from small agricultural producers grouped in cooperatives or producer organizations (for the most part) and from local suppliers or merchants. Local purchases stimulate the promotion of agricultural production and increased income for small producers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;WFP is working with the Government of Burundi to help building more robust food systems and resilient communities by working with farmers to increase the quantity and quality of their production and therefore increasing smallholder farmers&amp;rsquo; livelihoods; by purchasing food locally from smallholder farmers and creating market opportunities with the Home-Grown school feeding and program; by enhancing the production of special nutritious flour and milk across the country.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&#xA;The World Food Program has been present in Burundi in the education sector since just after Burundi's independence. Initially, it started assisting secondary school students where boarding schools received food aid for all children in the schools concerned. Within the framework of the Millennium Development Goals in its objective of primary education for all, WFP Burundi started the school feeding program in primary and pre-schools. &#xA;&#xA;Following the food insecurity in the province o</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
  </rdf:Description>
 </rdf:RDF>
</x:xmpmeta>

